00:14:49,588 --> 00:14:53,488
So in Darwin Core and some of the other TDWG standards, there are terms that

255
00:14:53,608 --> 00:14:55,028
help to get at this problem,

256
00:14:55,948 --> 00:14:59,788
but they, like I said before, are not necessarily indexed

257
00:14:59,908 --> 00:15:03,808
or readily available for search. So we have the two new

258
00:15:03,868 --> 00:15:06,728
terms we've talked about. Darwin Core preparations is one that gets mentioned a

259
00:15:06,768 --> 00:15:10,148
lot, but if you're familiar with that term, you know it gets used in

260
00:15:10,408 --> 00:15:13,828
50 million different ways. It does not have a controlled

261
00:15:13,868 --> 00:15:17,728
vocabulary, and it's definitely not indexed in a way that's very

262
00:15:17,788 --> 00:15:18,268
searchable.

263
00:15:19,308 --> 00:15:21,588
Counts help you. Taxonomy, of course, helps you.

264
00:15:22,008 --> 00:15:25,028
One thing that we talk a lot about in the paleo community is the need for a

265
00:15:25,088 --> 00:15:27,328
morphology term that doesn't even exist yet.

266
00:15:28,068 --> 00:15:31,248
And then Latimer Core has two that would help as well, but those have not been

267
00:15:31,308 --> 00:15:32,528
adopted into Darwin Core.

268
00:15:33,548 --> 00:15:37,148
So using the material entity type really answers this question for

269
00:15:37,228 --> 00:15:41,068
us in an initial search and filter for when you're trying to find the

270
00:15:41,128 --> 00:15:42,868
right fossil records for your

271
00:15:43,768 --> 00:15:44,228
collection

272
00:15:45,028 --> 00:15:48,748
or for your search. So we have a QR code

273
00:15:48,828 --> 00:15:50,768
here for a document that outlines

274
00:15:51,768 --> 00:15:53,788
the vocabulary terms that we're proposing.

275
00:15:54,388 --> 00:15:56,368
It's just this initial set. We do

276
00:15:57,608 --> 00:16:00,968
think that this is likely going to be a hierarchical

277
00:16:01,048 --> 00:16:03,088
vocabulary with two concept levels.

278
00:16:03,168 --> 00:16:05,028
So what we're proposing right now is just the

279
00:16:06,248 --> 00:16:07,268
first level of

280
00:16:08,508 --> 00:16:12,308
values, and then nested under that is probably a

281
00:16:12,368 --> 00:16:16,328
more informal list of terms that could be used to be more specific within those if

282
00:16:16,448 --> 00:16:20,328
you want to do that, if you want to adopt it to that level, or you need that

283
00:16:20,368 --> 00:16:21,448
within your own system.

284
00:16:22,428 --> 00:16:26,388
And so those level two are included just to give some context and help understand

285
00:16:26,448 --> 00:16:27,428
how we organize this

286
00:16:28,248 --> 00:16:31,348
in the document. And with that, I will leave you with

287
00:16:32,868 --> 00:16:34,828
our-- Is this two minutes? Okay, good.

288
00:16:36,648 --> 00:16:38,008
I thought you were holding up the end sign.

289
00:16:38,988 --> 00:16:42,648
So this is, again, the list of terms that we're proposing with images from the talk

290
00:16:42,728 --> 00:16:45,448
of how we would align those images to those terms.

291
00:16:47,388 --> 00:16:50,748
And I'm happy to leave it with questions there.

