00:18:49,158 --> 00:18:52,518
is Teresa Miller, who's going to be talking about extending Specify

330
00:18:53,558 --> 00:18:55,958
7 for geology collections. Welcome, Teresa.

331
00:18:57,838 --> 00:18:57,998
Thank you.

332
00:18:58,038 --> 00:18:58,458
Do you have a mic?

333
00:18:59,078 --> 00:18:59,598
Yes, I do.

334
00:18:59,598 --> 00:18:59,878
Oh, yes. Thank you.

335
00:19:00,718 --> 00:19:03,338
Oh, speakers, just make sure you get mic'd up before your talk.

336
00:19:04,438 --> 00:19:06,458
That's how good the mic is. You couldn't even tell I had it on.

337
00:19:08,078 --> 00:19:10,918
Okay. Hello, everyone. My name is Teresa Miller with the Specify Collections

338
00:19:10,978 --> 00:19:14,138
Consortium. I am here today to talk about GeoSpecify,

339
00:19:16,538 --> 00:19:17,798
which is a collaborative initiative

340
00:19:18,918 --> 00:19:19,538
between

341
00:19:21,418 --> 00:19:25,098
curators, collection managers, data programmers, all to

342
00:19:25,158 --> 00:19:29,037
try to extend the existing Specify data model to support

343
00:19:29,098 --> 00:19:30,498
geology collections as well.

344
00:19:31,938 --> 00:19:34,558
I'm just going to move on. The idea was

345
00:19:35,718 --> 00:19:38,918
that we wouldn't create a separate system to support-- I'm just going to move away

346
00:19:38,958 --> 00:19:42,438
from that, a separate system to support geology, but it would be

347
00:19:42,478 --> 00:19:44,658
incorporated into the existing data model.

348
00:19:44,758 --> 00:19:48,738
So natural history collections and earth science collections could all use the

349
00:19:48,838 --> 00:19:52,778
same system. So it's all in the same platform, all in the same

350
00:19:53,018 --> 00:19:56,658
standards, so everybody in your institution could be in the same

351
00:19:56,738 --> 00:19:57,198
system.

352
00:20:01,298 --> 00:20:02,598
Oh, it's showing me the future one. Okay.

353
00:20:03,358 --> 00:20:07,318
So our project was led by Beta Hofmann

354
00:20:07,418 --> 00:20:11,278
at the Natural History Museum of Bern, James Beach at the Specify

355
00:20:11,338 --> 00:20:13,618
Collections Consortium, and Ben Norton.

356
00:20:14,898 --> 00:20:18,818
It was funded by the Swiss Academy of Sciences and their SwissCollnNet grant.

357
00:20:19,958 --> 00:20:23,778
It was done in two phases. The first phase was led by Ben Norton, started

358
00:20:23,858 --> 00:20:27,778
in 2023, and the idea of his phase was to come up with a

359
00:20:27,818 --> 00:20:30,538
conceptual data model based on real workflows.

360
00:20:31,218 --> 00:20:32,217
So he worked with

361
00:20:33,078 --> 00:20:37,018
collections throughout Switzerland to try to come up with their requirements and

362
00:20:37,058 --> 00:20:40,218
their needs. He also came up with a classification system.

363
00:20:41,078 --> 00:20:44,058
Phase two, led by the Specify Collections Consortium,

364
00:20:44,918 --> 00:20:48,558
worked on really defining the requirements for such a data model.

365
00:20:48,978 --> 00:20:52,858
What are the actual workflows? Going and getting use cases from different

366
00:20:52,918 --> 00:20:56,358
people throughout Switzerland and throughout the wider community to

367
00:20:56,438 --> 00:20:59,718
see what their actual needs are. How do they use specimens?

368
00:20:59,798 --> 00:21:01,418
How do they perceive of specimens?

369
00:21:02,258 --> 00:21:05,778
And then with that, we come up with a design and implementation

370
00:21:05,878 --> 00:21:07,098
for the data model.

371
00:21:09,798 --> 00:21:13,318
We worked with the Swiss collaborative team, which had representatives

372
00:21:13,378 --> 00:21:14,398
from four

373
00:21:15,218 --> 00:21:18,538
natural history collections, or four collections throughout

374
00:21:18,598 --> 00:21:22,098
Switzerland in Bern, Basel, Geneva, and Lausanne.

375
00:21:23,358 --> 00:21:27,258
We met with the team regularly to really talk to them and get

376
00:21:27,278 --> 00:21:30,818
their feedback, hear from them how they work with collections,

377
00:21:31,238 --> 00:21:34,038
hear from them what their concerns were and what their needs were.

378
00:21:35,278 --> 00:21:38,938
Then we went to the broader community with our GeoSpecify advisory

379
00:21:38,978 --> 00:21:42,278
committee. These are representatives from not only members of the Specify

380
00:21:42,338 --> 00:21:46,078
Collections Consortium, but the community at large, to

381
00:21:46,138 --> 00:21:49,398
really get a more diverse set of needs and requirements from

382
00:21:50,138 --> 00:21:52,678
all people working with geoscience collections.

383
00:21:56,158 --> 00:22:00,138
In addition to that, we did, I say we, I wasn't involved, I

384
00:22:00,158 --> 00:22:03,598
didn't get to go, an intensive requirements gathering trip

385
00:22:04,258 --> 00:22:08,038
where members of the Specify team got to go to the four

386
00:22:08,118 --> 00:22:08,558
major

387
00:22:09,498 --> 00:22:13,378
history museums throughout Switzerland and really talk to the

388
00:22:13,438 --> 00:22:17,358
people there, get into the collections, see how they work with the collections, see

389
00:22:17,378 --> 00:22:21,238
what their needs were, talk to them about how they do classification and what their

390
00:22:21,278 --> 00:22:24,818
needs are. How do they do searches on the different

391
00:22:24,898 --> 00:22:25,418
times?

392
00:22:26,758 --> 00:22:29,598
When they're cataloging something, what are the things that they need to be able to

393
00:22:29,678 --> 00:22:32,878
catalog? They need to be able to warn their people that something's heavy.

394
00:22:32,918 --> 00:22:35,198
They need to be able to warn them that it's toxic.

395
00:22:35,738 --> 00:22:38,838
So all of those things went into our design.

396
00:22:41,917 --> 00:22:45,297
So with that, we extended Specify in several key ways.

397
00:22:45,797 --> 00:22:47,638
So let's start by looking at the trees.

398
00:22:47,878 --> 00:22:50,598
I'm going to apologize now, I've never done this in under 20 minutes, so I'm trying

399
00:22:50,618 --> 00:22:53,718
to rush. So Godspeed to us all. So

400
00:22:54,938 --> 00:22:56,678
Specify already has six

401
00:22:56,738 --> 00:23:00,718
different trees, and these are

402
00:23:00,918 --> 00:23:03,298
visual representations of hierarchical data.

403
00:23:04,198 --> 00:23:07,858
For GeoSpecify, we really focused on three of these trees.

404
00:23:08,678 --> 00:23:12,478
So we start with the chronostratigraphy tree, which Specify has

405
00:23:12,558 --> 00:23:15,498
always had, but we extended under GeoSpecify.

406
00:23:15,938 --> 00:23:18,338
And I'm really excited to talk about this because last time I explained it, it was

407
00:23:18,418 --> 00:23:20,738
all entomologists and botanists, they didn't care.

408
00:23:21,358 --> 00:23:25,138
So they didn't understand how cool this is, that you can now see the end and

409
00:23:25,218 --> 00:23:27,438
start date in chronostratigraphy.

410
00:23:27,758 --> 00:23:31,178
So when you're looking at the tree, you really know what the dates are that you're

411
00:23:31,218 --> 00:23:35,098
working with. There's a cool little button you can click, and it will

412
00:23:35,158 --> 00:23:38,918
bring up the international chronostratigraphic chart, so you can see the chart when

413
00:23:38,938 --> 00:23:40,878
you're entering the data and really understand it.

414
00:23:41,418 --> 00:23:45,158
The tree is always organized by age, so when you're going through it, you have a

415
00:23:45,218 --> 00:23:47,578
feeling of where everything is in the age.

416
00:23:48,258 --> 00:23:52,198
In addition to that, there's a little checkbox at the bottom, so you

417
00:23:52,218 --> 00:23:55,398
can document chronostratigraphy and biostratigraphy in the same

418
00:23:55,438 --> 00:23:58,958
chronostratigraphy tree. And then when you're searching it, you can say, "Actually,

419
00:23:59,058 --> 00:24:02,338
I only want to see biostratigraphy nodes," and then it will just show you those

420
00:24:02,418 --> 00:24:06,198
nodes in your tree. "I want to see everything," it'll show you everything in the

421
00:24:06,278 --> 00:24:09,718
tree. And then there's a lot of other cool features for the tree, but I can't

422
00:24:10,318 --> 00:24:13,658
explain them to you because we don't have time, that allow you to see the objects

423
00:24:13,698 --> 00:24:14,998
associated and so on.

424
00:24:16,038 --> 00:24:17,238
For lithostratigraphy,

425
00:24:18,198 --> 00:24:19,998
this is another tree that we've always had.

426
00:24:20,978 --> 00:24:22,038
This is a tree that

427
00:24:22,958 --> 00:24:26,858
each individual institution can add their own data to, because

428
00:24:26,918 --> 00:24:30,798
each region is so different and so unique with their own terms and their

429
00:24:30,898 --> 00:24:31,158
own

430
00:24:32,434 --> 00:24:34,854
Lithology, so you can enter that for yourselves.

431
00:24:36,594 --> 00:24:40,494
And then Tectonic Unit was added for GeoSpecify so that

432
00:24:40,554 --> 00:24:44,154
you can really document the tectonic unit of your region.

433
00:24:44,214 --> 00:24:48,174
So each institution can enter their own values since it does differ so

434
00:24:48,214 --> 00:24:49,574
widely between regions.

435
00:24:51,714 --> 00:24:55,034
Then let's move on to the tables. Several new tables were added.

436
00:24:56,034 --> 00:24:59,554
We'll start with Geologic Context, which was already there a little bit, but we

437
00:24:59,614 --> 00:25:03,394
extended it for GeoSpecify. Now, this is really the

438
00:25:03,454 --> 00:25:06,234
backbone of geological description in Specify.

439
00:25:06,274 --> 00:25:09,074
This is the one table that links out to all of the

440
00:25:09,154 --> 00:25:11,194
geo-references. So

441
00:25:12,414 --> 00:25:15,994
you can link out to your lithostrat here, you can link out to your

442
00:25:16,054 --> 00:25:19,554
chronostrat, your tectonic unit, and

443
00:25:19,974 --> 00:25:23,914
biostratigraphy. And the really cool thing is you can also have a start period

444
00:25:23,974 --> 00:25:25,954
and end period for your chronostratigraphy.

445
00:25:26,554 --> 00:25:30,174
So if your object spans a range,

446
00:25:30,614 --> 00:25:33,854
you can document that here and really say it goes from here to here.

447
00:25:36,493 --> 00:25:37,474
We also added

448
00:25:38,754 --> 00:25:42,554
Relative and Absolute Age tables, and we added this as a one-to-many,

449
00:25:42,954 --> 00:25:46,474
meaning that you can have multiple ages for each object.

450
00:25:46,974 --> 00:25:50,914
So you can have multiple relative ages, you can have multiple absolute ages

451
00:25:50,974 --> 00:25:54,914
for one object. And I always remember Beta really

452
00:25:54,994 --> 00:25:58,734
focusing on this when we were talking to him, because one object

453
00:25:58,794 --> 00:26:02,694
might have an age of deposition, it might have an age of metamorphism, it

454
00:26:02,734 --> 00:26:06,334
might have multiple ages, and if you can't document all of that

455
00:26:06,394 --> 00:26:10,094
information, you really lose the context for the object and for the

456
00:26:10,134 --> 00:26:13,834
matrix it was in. So this allows you to document all of that and keep

457
00:26:13,894 --> 00:26:14,854
track of everything.

458
00:26:17,334 --> 00:26:20,314
But now that you have multiple ages, what do you do with it?

459
00:26:20,714 --> 00:26:23,034
And this is my favorite tool, so I'm going to spend more time on it.

460
00:26:23,834 --> 00:26:27,814
So we have extended age queries. Now, extended age queries

461
00:26:27,914 --> 00:26:30,674
allow you to search all of that at one time.

462
00:26:31,414 --> 00:26:35,394
So I mentioned we have the geologic context, and you could put

463
00:26:35,434 --> 00:26:39,094
chronostratigraphy there. Well, you can also put chronostratigraphy in

464
00:26:39,154 --> 00:26:42,414
relative age. But when you're searching, you don't want to search two fields.

465
00:26:43,013 --> 00:26:44,974
Extended age query allows you to search in one,

466
00:26:46,094 --> 00:26:49,454
which is really cool. So it looks at both of those, extended age,

467
00:26:50,714 --> 00:26:51,794
absolute age, and

468
00:26:52,634 --> 00:26:55,294
geologic context for the age you're looking for.

469
00:26:55,754 --> 00:26:59,354
And if that wasn't cool enough, on top of that, you can

470
00:26:59,454 --> 00:27:03,094
add the name. So I could add Jurassic, and it would

471
00:27:03,134 --> 00:27:06,274
transfer that automatically to an age range, so

472
00:27:07,974 --> 00:27:11,674
a time range. So when I say Jurassic, it's actually going to look at the range

473
00:27:11,734 --> 00:27:15,154
associated with that. Or I could be very specific

474
00:27:15,734 --> 00:27:19,634
and put in a range. I could put in something like 15 million years ago, and it will

475
00:27:19,674 --> 00:27:22,134
search that, and it will transfer that to name as well.

476
00:27:22,214 --> 00:27:25,174
So it will consider that when it's doing the search.

477
00:27:26,114 --> 00:27:29,934
I can also put in a range, so I can say 15 million to five million

478
00:27:29,954 --> 00:27:32,794
years ago, and it will search for everything within that range.

479
00:27:34,954 --> 00:27:37,194
But wait, there's more, because this is my favorite tool.

480
00:27:37,574 --> 00:27:41,054
So in addition to that, you can do it strict or non-strict,

481
00:27:41,754 --> 00:27:45,574
meaning if I do it strict, I said 15 million to five million years

482
00:27:45,634 --> 00:27:48,534
ago. Ten million fits in that range.

483
00:27:48,874 --> 00:27:52,614
But if I have an object that's 10 million to two million, that doesn't fit in the

484
00:27:52,674 --> 00:27:56,054
range. So that's not going to come back because I didn't want that back.

485
00:27:56,114 --> 00:27:57,554
I don't want things from two million years ago.

486
00:27:58,054 --> 00:28:00,574
If I do non-strict, it will bring back that data.

487
00:28:01,034 --> 00:28:03,834
So I can bring back things that go over boundaries if I want it to.

488
00:28:04,594 --> 00:28:06,794
So an extremely cool tool.

489
00:28:09,514 --> 00:28:12,534
So let's move on to functionality because I only have five minutes left, and this

490
00:28:12,594 --> 00:28:15,674
is the stuff that my boss really likes, so I really need to focus on this.

491
00:28:16,094 --> 00:28:17,634
So Collection Object Types,

492
00:28:18,714 --> 00:28:22,494
I will admit, is also cool. So Collection Object Types allow you to have different

493
00:28:22,554 --> 00:28:26,354
types of objects in the same collection because as you

494
00:28:26,414 --> 00:28:26,714
know,

495
00:28:27,574 --> 00:28:31,454
minerals have different requirements from rocks, have different requirements from

496
00:28:31,534 --> 00:28:33,874
paleo. All of them are very different.

497
00:28:34,354 --> 00:28:37,574
So you're going to have different attributes that you want to document, different

498
00:28:37,614 --> 00:28:41,494
classification trees. You might have different numbering sequences as well.

499
00:28:42,194 --> 00:28:45,174
So Types allows you to document all of that.

500
00:28:45,574 --> 00:28:49,174
So you can have a specific type and say this type uses this

501
00:28:49,234 --> 00:28:52,734
classification tree. It also uses these forms,

502
00:28:53,533 --> 00:28:55,634
and it also uses this numbering sequence.

503
00:28:56,474 --> 00:29:00,414
Or you could say, "Actually, all of mine use the same numbering sequence," so they

504
00:29:00,454 --> 00:29:03,114
all have different forms, but they use the same numbering sequence.

505
00:29:03,674 --> 00:29:06,754
So it gives you a lot of variation in how you document things.

506
00:29:07,294 --> 00:29:10,854
But it keeps everything in the same collection because as you know,

507
00:29:11,294 --> 00:29:14,394
it often has paleo items and

508
00:29:15,194 --> 00:29:18,374
rocks and minerals all in the same collection. You're responsible for all of those.

509
00:29:18,914 --> 00:29:21,994
So it keeps everything together. Sorry, I'm going to rush through because

510
00:29:22,774 --> 00:29:23,794
probably only have four minutes left.

511
00:29:24,214 --> 00:29:24,374
So

512
00:29:25,674 --> 00:29:27,474
in addition to that, we have new default forms.

513
00:29:28,214 --> 00:29:31,534
So when you first get into Specify, we have these forms that

514
00:29:31,614 --> 00:29:34,874
automatically have the fields that you're looking for, for, say,

515
00:29:34,934 --> 00:29:38,354
mineralogy. So these are basic fields with drop-down

516
00:29:38,394 --> 00:29:40,994
lists that make sense for that discipline type.

517
00:29:41,574 --> 00:29:44,454
You can modify these, you can edit it, you can change things around,

518
00:29:45,214 --> 00:29:47,934
but you can start from a point that

519
00:29:48,774 --> 00:29:50,094
the community has agreed on.

