00:29:51,474 --> 00:29:52,594
Moving on to COGs.

521
00:29:53,654 --> 00:29:57,094
So Collection Object Groups are, as they say, groups of collection

522
00:29:57,194 --> 00:30:01,154
objects. So you can have multiple objects that are documented and cataloged in

523
00:30:01,194 --> 00:30:03,754
your collection, and groups will put them together.

524
00:30:04,174 --> 00:30:05,654
And so this is what it might look like.

525
00:30:06,074 --> 00:30:06,954
We have one group that

526
00:30:07,934 --> 00:30:11,234
has multiple objects in there. They can be different types.

527
00:30:11,454 --> 00:30:15,214
You can see fossils and minerals and rock all in the same group.

528
00:30:17,214 --> 00:30:19,014
We have three different types of this.

529
00:30:19,414 --> 00:30:23,094
We have Discrete, which are individual items which are separated.

530
00:30:23,514 --> 00:30:27,494
We have Consolidated, which means everything's on the same slab, and

531
00:30:27,514 --> 00:30:29,674
we have Drill Cores, which are drill cores.

532
00:30:30,454 --> 00:30:32,934
So Discrete means objects that

533
00:30:34,066 --> 00:30:35,986
might have been together originally.

534
00:30:36,206 --> 00:30:39,546
They were part of the same dinosaur, or they were part of the same slab originally,

535
00:30:39,606 --> 00:30:43,226
but they got broken apart. So they're not actually connected now that they're in

536
00:30:43,286 --> 00:30:43,986
your collection.

537
00:30:45,406 --> 00:30:48,106
Consolidated means it's all the same object.

538
00:30:48,526 --> 00:30:52,486
So it's all in this one specimen that has minerals and has rocks

539
00:30:52,746 --> 00:30:54,906
and has a fossil in it.

540
00:30:55,866 --> 00:30:56,066
So

541
00:30:56,986 --> 00:30:59,846
it's all considered one thing and is stored together.

542
00:31:01,706 --> 00:31:05,506
These can share collection object or catalog numbers, or they can have

543
00:31:05,566 --> 00:31:08,146
unique catalog numbers, depending on how you want to use it.

544
00:31:09,726 --> 00:31:10,386
Components

545
00:31:11,526 --> 00:31:15,106
let you get even more granular. So components allow you to

546
00:31:15,166 --> 00:31:19,006
document different categorizations or identifications

547
00:31:19,346 --> 00:31:22,986
within the same specimen. So it's one rock, but I want to document all the

548
00:31:23,006 --> 00:31:24,526
different minerals that are in there.

549
00:31:25,246 --> 00:31:28,606
So I want to mark that there's quartz in there, and I want to mark that there's

550
00:31:28,646 --> 00:31:31,726
feldspar in there. I don't know if those ever go together, but you want to mark all

551
00:31:31,766 --> 00:31:35,706
of that together. You can do it as basic as a type,

552
00:31:35,926 --> 00:31:39,586
which tells you what tree to go with, or you can get really complex and say, "I

553
00:31:39,626 --> 00:31:42,366
want to document the percentage that's in this rock.

554
00:31:42,606 --> 00:31:46,526
I want to document the colors. I want to document the attributes about this."

555
00:31:47,066 --> 00:31:49,826
So it allows you to do whatever's right for your workflow.

556
00:31:51,446 --> 00:31:55,006
And you can inherit numbers for those, or you can not assign numbers.

