1060
01:03:25,436 --> 01:03:28,836
So to start off, what is SESR? SESR is a large database

1061
01:03:29,676 --> 01:03:32,856
of metadata records describing physical samples and specimens.

1062
01:03:33,436 --> 01:03:37,196
And these are physical samples and specimens both from curated museum collections

1063
01:03:37,236 --> 01:03:41,116
or core repositories or things like that, as well as samples and specimens from

1064
01:03:41,176 --> 01:03:42,716
people's personal collections.

1065
01:03:43,876 --> 01:03:47,456
SESR's been around for almost 20 years, plus or minus.

1066
01:03:47,856 --> 01:03:49,136
It was initially founded in

1067
01:03:50,216 --> 01:03:53,776
2004 by Kirsten Leonard, and it's been fully operational

1068
01:03:54,056 --> 01:03:57,216
since 2009. Kirsten Leonard is the PI of this project.

1069
01:03:57,376 --> 01:03:58,636
She's at Columbia University.

1070
01:03:59,556 --> 01:04:03,276
SESR is also the birthplace of the IGSN, which is a globally unique persistent

1071
01:04:03,356 --> 01:04:07,136
identifier for samples, and I'll talk a little bit more about what that means in

1072
01:04:07,176 --> 01:04:07,756
the next slide.

1073
01:04:08,716 --> 01:04:12,356
And while SESR's name says Earth and Extraterrestrial

1074
01:04:12,416 --> 01:04:12,816
Samples,

1075
01:04:13,816 --> 01:04:14,116
we

1076
01:04:14,976 --> 01:04:16,536
host a wide range of sample types

1077
01:04:17,636 --> 01:04:20,956
beyond those domains as well. So we have a growing number of

1078
01:04:20,996 --> 01:04:23,496
biodiversity records that are stored in SESR,

1079
01:04:24,456 --> 01:04:27,136
and even some medical records as well.

