1091
01:05:11,176 --> 01:05:15,056
Like I said before, one of SESR's main functionalities is that it mints identifiers

1092
01:05:15,116 --> 01:05:19,056
for samples. So it's a place where you can go to get a permanent, unique identifier

1093
01:05:19,116 --> 01:05:20,536
for your samples or specimens.

1094
01:05:22,056 --> 01:05:25,556
The identifier we use is the IGSN, so that's the International Generic Sample

1095
01:05:25,636 --> 01:05:29,076
Number, formerly the International Geo Sample Number.

1096
01:05:30,296 --> 01:05:31,656
This is essentially a DOI.

1097
01:05:33,176 --> 01:05:36,656
While it wasn't always a DOI, DataCite took over management over the last couple of

1098
01:05:36,696 --> 01:05:38,496
years, and it got converted into a DOI.

1099
01:05:40,176 --> 01:05:43,836
There's a couple of advantages of using an IGSN for your samples as opposed

1100
01:05:43,896 --> 01:05:45,596
to just a catalog number,

1101
01:05:46,476 --> 01:05:47,636
which I'll talk about

1102
01:05:48,596 --> 01:05:52,256
in this talk. But first and foremost, it ensures that the number you're assigning

1103
01:05:52,296 --> 01:05:54,336
to your sample is completely unique.

1104
01:05:54,736 --> 01:05:58,136
And when you use that number in a paper or in a data set, you can

1105
01:05:58,216 --> 01:06:02,116
unambiguously link your research products back to the sample or specimen

1106
01:06:02,176 --> 01:06:05,716
from which they came. So this makes it a lot easier for people to find

1107
01:06:05,776 --> 01:06:06,996
samples to reuse.

1108
01:06:07,856 --> 01:06:11,016
And also it makes it easier for you to show the impact of your collections or the

1109
01:06:11,076 --> 01:06:14,536
impact of the samples by finding the network of

1110
01:06:14,576 --> 01:06:16,256
resources that they're connected to.

