1151
01:08:40,681 --> 01:08:41,420
and things like that.

1152
01:08:43,440 --> 01:08:47,200
So how does SESR support digital extended specimens?

1153
01:08:47,721 --> 01:08:51,360
So the most unique thing about SESR is that it mints

1154
01:08:51,420 --> 01:08:55,280
PIDs. It mints these unique identifiers that are guaranteed to be globally

1155
01:08:55,360 --> 01:08:55,700
unique.

1156
01:08:56,800 --> 01:08:58,740
This makes it possible to

1157
01:09:00,620 --> 01:09:04,021
build out that chain of related digital

1158
01:09:04,080 --> 01:09:08,061
resources related to a sample in a more persistent and more robust

1159
01:09:08,420 --> 01:09:08,681
way.

1160
01:09:09,500 --> 01:09:13,101
So some of the core things, information that we store in SESR

1161
01:09:13,160 --> 01:09:16,820
metadata records, so sample metadata records, are first, that

1162
01:09:16,900 --> 01:09:20,641
IGSN. Second, we also store other identifiers and catalog numbers in the

1163
01:09:20,681 --> 01:09:24,639
metadata. So if there's a field number or a local catalog number, that's one of

1164
01:09:24,681 --> 01:09:26,699
the first things that we ask for in the metadata.

1165
01:09:27,160 --> 01:09:30,800
And we also have explicit semantically rich connections to related digital

1166
01:09:30,860 --> 01:09:34,601
resources in the metadata. So that screenshot on the right-hand side is showing two

1167
01:09:34,660 --> 01:09:37,561
linked resources to a sample.

1168
01:09:38,740 --> 01:09:42,641
But not only do those live in the metadata on SESR,

1169
01:09:42,780 --> 01:09:45,360
they also get propagated out to DataCite,

1170
01:09:46,221 --> 01:09:50,061
which gives you additional functionality and visualization tools to see

1171
01:09:50,120 --> 01:09:51,920
the network of related resources here.

1172
01:09:52,000 --> 01:09:53,420
So on the left, you see this little

1173
01:09:54,860 --> 01:09:58,600
ball and spoke diagram here. This is on DataCite's Data

1174
01:09:58,660 --> 01:10:02,240
Commons webpage. And DataCite is making it

1175
01:10:02,300 --> 01:10:06,040
possible for you to traverse the network of samples and related

1176
01:10:06,100 --> 01:10:07,140
digital materials

1177
01:10:08,860 --> 01:10:12,840
through the DataCite Commons. And DataCite also provides some of its

1178
01:10:12,920 --> 01:10:16,840
own metrics that you can search through, such as number of times cited, things

1179
01:10:16,860 --> 01:10:20,320
like that. So by using the IGSN, by using this PID,

1180
01:10:21,240 --> 01:10:24,640
it makes it possible to store all of this related information.

1181
01:10:24,840 --> 01:10:28,600
And by propagating it to DataCite, it makes it available for other people

1182
01:10:28,680 --> 01:10:32,360
to harvest and improve on that chain

1183
01:10:32,500 --> 01:10:36,180
of related resources and showing the

1184
01:10:36,240 --> 01:10:40,160
impact and network of your sample and the work that has come out of them.

1185
01:10:42,180 --> 01:10:46,040
One of the major improvements that we've made to SESR in this migration is that

1186
01:10:46,080 --> 01:10:50,040
we've updated and enhanced the vocabularies that we're using to describe

1187
01:10:50,100 --> 01:10:53,460
samples. So we're now making use of the iSamples

1188
01:10:53,520 --> 01:10:55,520
vocabularies to describe samples,

1189
01:10:56,460 --> 01:10:58,760
which hopefully increases interoperability.

1190
01:11:00,100 --> 01:11:04,040
So we're using the Earth and environmental science extension of the iSamples

1191
01:11:04,100 --> 01:11:07,940
material vocabulary. This was designed by Steve Richard, and we're

1192
01:11:08,000 --> 01:11:10,320
using a light application profile of it.

1193
01:11:10,380 --> 01:11:11,800
We've lightly edited it to

1194
01:11:13,580 --> 01:11:17,560
pare down the vocabulary so that it's easier to use by our users.

1195
01:11:18,660 --> 01:11:22,600
But this is a much expanded vocabulary, using a

1196
01:11:22,660 --> 01:11:25,720
couple of thousand terms as opposed to just a couple of hundred, which is what we

1197
01:11:25,740 --> 01:11:26,580
were using before.

1198
01:11:27,600 --> 01:11:31,360
And this should hopefully make it possible, A, for people to better

1199
01:11:31,420 --> 01:11:32,520
describe the samples.

1200
01:11:33,540 --> 01:11:36,540
B, it should make it easier for people to search for samples and find the very

1201
01:11:36,600 --> 01:11:38,600
specific kinds of samples that they're looking for.

1202
01:11:39,100 --> 01:11:43,000
And C, it should improve the interoperability between SESR and any

1203
01:11:43,080 --> 01:11:45,400
other system that's using these vocabularies.

1204
01:11:47,320 --> 01:11:50,680
SESR's new architecture also better supports the development of extension

1205
01:11:50,720 --> 01:11:54,500
vocabularies and fields. So basically, the new database

1206
01:11:55,200 --> 01:11:58,660
design makes it easier for us to create forms and

1207
01:11:58,900 --> 01:12:02,680
controlled vocabularies for other domains that are not currently using

1208
01:12:02,740 --> 01:12:06,180
SESR as extensively. So right now, we're developing a crosswalk

1209
01:12:06,220 --> 01:12:08,640
from SESR's schema to Darwin Core,

1210
01:12:09,640 --> 01:12:13,460
and also an application profile of sample-related fields from

1211
01:12:13,500 --> 01:12:17,020
Darwin Core so that we can better support biodiversity collections.

1212
01:12:17,060 --> 01:12:21,040
So we're working with someone at the Illinois Natural History Survey to register

1213
01:12:21,080 --> 01:12:22,640
some of their herpetology collection.

1214
01:12:23,000 --> 01:12:25,980
And through that work, we're hoping that we can better support other biodiversity

1215
01:12:26,020 --> 01:12:26,900
collections as well.

1216
01:12:27,780 --> 01:12:31,480
In the future, we're also developing profiles for marine and lacustrine

1217
01:12:31,560 --> 01:12:32,140
sampling

1218
01:12:33,480 --> 01:12:36,520
communities, so basically people who are working in core repositories.

1219
01:12:37,960 --> 01:12:41,740
New profiles for astromaterials and meteorites, and finally, for

1220
01:12:41,840 --> 01:12:43,960
tephras, for the tephra community.

1221
01:12:46,240 --> 01:12:49,920
Finally, I want to note that I'm the PI of two active grants that aim to improve

1222
01:12:49,960 --> 01:12:53,500
how we share and connect sample metadata in the earth sciences and beyond.

1223
01:12:53,700 --> 01:12:57,400
One is through the NSF PHAROS program, and the other is from the

1224
01:12:57,740 --> 01:13:00,680
IMLS, which is the Institute for Museum and Library Services.

1225
01:13:01,500 --> 01:13:04,280
In both of these, we're doing things like running hackathons, running a webinar

1226
01:13:04,340 --> 01:13:08,080
series, developing tutorials on curating sample metadata, and also

1227
01:13:08,120 --> 01:13:11,840
developing roadmaps for researchers, scholarly publishers,

1228
01:13:12,000 --> 01:13:15,640
data repositories, and sample repositories to

1229
01:13:15,680 --> 01:13:19,320
improve their metadata curation practices and

1230
01:13:19,360 --> 01:13:22,780
metadata sharing practices to make it easier to

1231
01:13:23,480 --> 01:13:26,200
connect samples with the things that we make from samples.

