05:50:52,214 --> 05:50:53,834
digitization project to date.

3790
05:50:55,234 --> 05:50:58,794
The pinned insect conveyor requires a multi-step workflow, which includes

3791
05:50:58,854 --> 05:51:02,394
pre-curation, imaging, and a virtual data-driven workflow.

3792
05:51:06,214 --> 05:51:09,994
Before a specimen sets foot on the conveyor, it goes through pre-curation

3793
05:51:10,034 --> 05:51:10,514
process.

3794
05:51:11,654 --> 05:51:15,434
We choose a taxonomic group, and then we will image that entire genus.

3795
05:51:16,334 --> 05:51:20,054
Taxonomy is vetted and necessary updates are made to our collection management

3796
05:51:20,114 --> 05:51:21,174
system called EMu.

3797
05:51:22,154 --> 05:51:25,614
Then we run a crystal report to create new header labels for each

3798
05:51:25,674 --> 05:51:26,794
individual unit tray.

3799
05:51:27,694 --> 05:51:29,994
These header labels serve many purposes.

3800
05:51:30,594 --> 05:51:33,094
First, they identify the species that is in the tray.

3801
05:51:34,354 --> 05:51:38,034
Second, they assign a unique identifier so that we can trace back

3802
05:51:38,834 --> 05:51:41,574
the specimen to the exact unit tray it came from.

3803
05:51:42,154 --> 05:51:42,654
Finally,

3804
05:51:44,314 --> 05:51:48,234
the taxonomy is embedded in the QR code, which allows it to be stored

3805
05:51:48,314 --> 05:51:52,234
in the images metadata. This streamlines the process of catalog

3806
05:51:52,274 --> 05:51:53,414
record creation later.

3807
05:51:54,454 --> 05:51:57,154
Unique drawer identifiers are also assigned at this point.

3808
05:51:57,694 --> 05:52:01,674
Drawer names are used to name the folders so that all specimen images within

3809
05:52:01,734 --> 05:52:05,114
a folder stay together. Once specimens have gone through

3810
05:52:05,154 --> 05:52:08,094
pre-curation, they are moved to the digitization suite.

3811
05:52:08,634 --> 05:52:12,114
I'd like to show you a quick time-lapse video of the conveyor in

3812
05:52:12,214 --> 05:52:12,674
action.

3813
05:52:19,034 --> 05:52:22,934
As you can see, black boats hold insects and they

3814
05:52:22,994 --> 05:52:26,734
move around the conveyor into the photography area.

3815
05:52:27,834 --> 05:52:31,014
Photography occurs inside that black box.

3816
05:52:35,514 --> 05:52:37,314
Conveyor operators can

3817
05:52:38,574 --> 05:52:41,094
image around 500 specimens per day.

3818
05:53:05,694 --> 05:53:09,094
As you just saw, specimens are prepared by the object handlers.

3819
05:53:09,534 --> 05:53:13,194
The insect labels are removed from the pin, and they're staged on a flat

3820
05:53:13,254 --> 05:53:17,094
surface. The specimen is placed also on a vertical stage.

3821
05:53:18,914 --> 05:53:22,094
At this time, they assign the specimen a unique identifier.

3822
05:53:22,294 --> 05:53:25,094
We typically call those a USNM barcode.

3823
05:53:26,854 --> 05:53:30,594
The pinned specimen is placed on the boat, and the boat goes

3824
05:53:30,674 --> 05:53:31,474
around the conveyor

3825
05:53:32,474 --> 05:53:33,654
until it is photographed.

3826
05:53:35,334 --> 05:53:39,234
All unique identifiers, meaning the unit tray, the drawer,

3827
05:53:40,094 --> 05:53:43,894
and the specimen ID, are scanned and become a part of the metadata for that

3828
05:53:43,974 --> 05:53:44,874
specimen's image.

3829
05:53:54,214 --> 05:53:57,954
As the specimen goes on the conveyor, a silhouette of the specimen is

3830
05:53:58,014 --> 05:54:01,774
taken to determine the imaging sphere, which determines the

3831
05:54:01,814 --> 05:54:02,494
resolution.

3832
05:54:04,374 --> 05:54:08,214
Three focus stacked images, about 30 slices each, are taken,

3833
05:54:08,354 --> 05:54:10,114
as well as an image of all the labels.

3834
05:54:10,874 --> 05:54:13,014
All metadata is stored in a JSON file.

