4671
06:45:28,302 --> 06:45:31,882
Today, I'll be speaking about digitizing heritage for science

4672
06:45:32,602 --> 06:45:36,362
and how the Medical Entomology Museum can function not

4673
06:45:36,442 --> 06:45:40,422
only as a repository of historical specimens, but as a

4674
06:45:40,482 --> 06:45:44,322
platform for biodiversity research and vector-borne

4675
06:45:44,382 --> 06:45:48,302
diseases surveillance. I will take you through the journey from

4676
06:45:48,362 --> 06:45:51,702
a physical specimen to structured digital data,

4677
06:45:52,382 --> 06:45:55,722
and ultimately to information that can be useful

4678
06:45:56,382 --> 06:45:58,322
for research and public health.

4679
06:46:02,942 --> 06:46:06,762
The Medical Entomology Museum has its roots in South Africa's early

4680
06:46:06,822 --> 06:46:10,062
response to malaria and other vector-borne diseases.

4681
06:46:10,782 --> 06:46:14,562
The Department of Entomology was established in 1925

4682
06:46:15,242 --> 06:46:19,082
under the headship of Dr. Alexander Inkela, initially

4683
06:46:19,142 --> 06:46:21,922
in response to public health needs. Dr.

4684
06:46:22,202 --> 06:46:26,002
Walter Desmier subsequently conducted extensive

4685
06:46:26,142 --> 06:46:30,102
surveys of mosquitoes in malaria-affected parts of the country.

4686
06:46:30,822 --> 06:46:34,742
These early activities generated specimens that documented

4687
06:46:34,802 --> 06:46:38,662
mosquito diversity, distribution, and disease vector

4688
06:46:38,722 --> 06:46:42,702
research. So when we look at this collection today, we are looking

4689
06:46:42,742 --> 06:46:45,282
at a century of scientific activity.

4690
06:46:46,162 --> 06:46:49,622
Digitization gives us an opportunity to take this historical

4691
06:46:49,742 --> 06:46:52,942
evidence and make it discoverable and

4692
06:46:53,022 --> 06:46:55,302
reusable for modern research.

4693
06:46:58,822 --> 06:47:01,502
So what exactly has been preserved over this century?

4694
06:47:02,202 --> 06:47:06,182
The museum currently preserves approximately 60,000 medically

4695
06:47:06,342 --> 06:47:10,322
important insect specimens, including around 12,000

4696
06:47:11,042 --> 06:47:12,342
Anopheles mosquitoes.

4697
06:47:13,242 --> 06:47:14,442
The collection includes

4698
06:47:14,682 --> 06:47:21,242
pinned

4699
06:47:21,362 --> 06:47:22,082
specimens,

4700
06:47:23,302 --> 06:47:24,942
specimens mounted on slides,

4701
06:47:26,302 --> 06:47:28,302
specimens preserved in different media,

4702
06:47:29,142 --> 06:47:33,082
historical collection, type material, and reference

4703
06:47:33,162 --> 06:47:33,762
vouchers.

4704
06:47:35,942 --> 06:47:39,262
Approximately 80% of our mosquito collection,

4705
06:47:39,882 --> 06:47:43,122
including the Anopheles records, have already been

4706
06:47:43,182 --> 06:47:47,062
digitized. I describe the physical collection as

4707
06:47:47,142 --> 06:47:51,102
scientific infrastructure because these are not simply objects

4708
06:47:51,162 --> 06:47:54,982
stored in cabinets. They are vouchers that can be

4709
06:47:55,022 --> 06:47:58,002
revisited for taxonomic verification,

4710
06:47:58,582 --> 06:48:02,182
morphological examination, imaging, and, where

4711
06:48:02,282 --> 06:48:04,342
appropriate, molecular research.

4712
06:48:08,782 --> 06:48:10,182
In this photograph here,

4713
06:48:11,202 --> 06:48:14,882
we show the pinned specimens of our collection in their original labels,

4714
06:48:15,562 --> 06:48:18,882
and this illustrates one of the most important concepts

4715
06:48:19,502 --> 06:48:21,842
behind our digitization program.

4716
06:48:22,602 --> 06:48:26,142
Every physical specimen in our collection is also

4717
06:48:26,202 --> 06:48:29,942
potentially a data point. The label may tell us the

4718
06:48:30,002 --> 06:48:30,562
taxon,

4719
06:48:31,782 --> 06:48:35,022
where the specimen was collected, when it was

4720
06:48:35,082 --> 06:48:39,022
collected, where it was collected, and sometimes the

4721
06:48:39,062 --> 06:48:42,562
collection method that was used or other information.

4722
06:48:43,041 --> 06:48:46,122
The specimen itself remains the voucher evidence.

4723
06:48:46,902 --> 06:48:50,802
When we digitize this information and connect it to the physical

4724
06:48:50,922 --> 06:48:54,882
specimen, we transform a museum object into a structured

4725
06:48:54,942 --> 06:48:58,722
biodiversity record. So digitization is not

4726
06:48:58,782 --> 06:49:01,722
simply about entering information into a spreadsheet.

4727
06:49:02,362 --> 06:49:05,802
It is about preserving the relationship between the

4728
06:49:05,922 --> 06:49:08,582
specimen and the scientific contents.

4729
06:49:09,202 --> 06:49:13,042
So the next question is: how do we make this historical record

4730
06:49:13,142 --> 06:49:13,622
usable?

4731
06:49:17,682 --> 06:49:21,462
In this slide, we summarize our digitization workflow.

4732
06:49:21,962 --> 06:49:25,882
We begin with inventory and accessioning so that the specimen

4733
06:49:25,942 --> 06:49:27,642
is properly accounted for.

4734
06:49:28,562 --> 06:49:32,402
We then transcribe the information from legacy labels and

4735
06:49:32,442 --> 06:49:35,602
records. The taxonomy has to be

4736
06:49:35,682 --> 06:49:39,542
standardized because names and classifications can change

4737
06:49:39,642 --> 06:49:43,282
over time. Historical locality information

4738
06:49:43,662 --> 06:49:47,042
must also be cleaned and, where possible, georeferenced.

4739
06:49:47,842 --> 06:49:51,662
The record now goes under quality control before

4740
06:49:51,702 --> 06:49:54,622
they are prepared for publication and integration.

4741
06:49:55,182 --> 06:49:57,762
The goal is to move from the FAIR principles.

4742
06:49:58,342 --> 06:50:01,302
FAIR means findable, accessible,

4743
06:50:02,022 --> 06:50:05,102
interoperable, and reusable. And

4744
06:50:05,162 --> 06:50:08,702
interoperability is particularly important for us.

4745
06:50:09,302 --> 06:50:12,722
We do not want to create a digital database that

4746
06:50:13,002 --> 06:50:15,882
simply becomes another isolated cabinet.

4747
06:50:16,382 --> 06:50:20,062
We want this data to eventually be able to move across

4748
06:50:20,122 --> 06:50:23,202
systems, disciplines, and generations.

4749
06:50:28,894 --> 06:50:32,693
Once specimen records are converted into structured data, we can

4750
06:50:32,774 --> 06:50:36,474
start asking questions that will be very difficult to answer by

4751
06:50:36,554 --> 06:50:38,634
simply looking through museum drawers.

4752
06:50:39,234 --> 06:50:42,474
We can analyze records across taxonomic,

4753
06:50:43,134 --> 06:50:45,334
geographical, and temporal dimensions.

4754
06:50:45,934 --> 06:50:49,874
For example, we can examine where particular mosquito or insect

4755
06:50:49,954 --> 06:50:53,454
specimens were historically recorded, how this

4756
06:50:53,674 --> 06:50:57,394
known distribution compares through time, or where there are

4757
06:50:57,454 --> 06:51:01,214
important sampling gaps. The image here you can see

4758
06:51:02,414 --> 06:51:05,814
also demonstrates the link between the collection, temporal research.

4759
06:51:06,414 --> 06:51:09,514
Here is the distribution mapping, morphological

4760
06:51:09,594 --> 06:51:13,514
identification where we identify the species, and laboratory

4761
06:51:13,614 --> 06:51:17,234
investigation. And an important advantage of the natural history

4762
06:51:17,354 --> 06:51:21,094
collection is that the physical voucher remains available.

4763
06:51:21,574 --> 06:51:25,234
So if an identification needs to be reconsidered, we can then

4764
06:51:25,274 --> 06:51:29,074
return to the specimen. That takes us from simple

4765
06:51:29,134 --> 06:51:32,274
digitizing records to actually generating

4766
06:51:32,773 --> 06:51:34,854
biodiversity intelligence.

4767
06:51:40,934 --> 06:51:43,054
Digitization should not be the endpoint.

4768
06:51:43,354 --> 06:51:46,794
The real scientific value comes when these records become usable

4769
06:51:46,934 --> 06:51:50,494
evidence. Digitized collections can provide a

4770
06:51:50,554 --> 06:51:53,634
historical baseline for distribution mapping,

4771
06:51:54,314 --> 06:51:56,714
temporal comparison, and trend analysis.

4772
06:51:57,314 --> 06:52:01,094
The example here you can see illustrates how entomological

4773
06:52:01,154 --> 06:52:05,014
data can contribute to scientific publications and malaria

4774
06:52:05,094 --> 06:52:08,974
vector surveillance outputs. Importantly, museum

4775
06:52:09,034 --> 06:52:11,614
data do not replace contemporary surveillance.

4776
06:52:12,174 --> 06:52:15,714
Instead, they provide historical context that can make

4777
06:52:16,034 --> 06:52:18,394
contemporary observation more meaningful.

4778
06:52:19,034 --> 06:52:22,714
If we know what was recorded in a locality several decades

4779
06:52:22,754 --> 06:52:26,614
ago, we have a stronger basis for interpreting what

4780
06:52:26,754 --> 06:52:28,194
we are observing there today.

4781
06:52:32,394 --> 06:52:35,814
This is where the work becomes particularly relevant to public health.

4782
06:52:36,414 --> 06:52:40,074
We can start with historical vouchers and ask, where was this

4783
06:52:40,134 --> 06:52:43,754
vector recorded before? We can use the physical collection for

4784
06:52:43,834 --> 06:52:47,754
taxonomy verification and ask, are we actually comparing the

4785
06:52:47,794 --> 06:52:51,674
same species? We can then combine that information with current

4786
06:52:51,774 --> 06:52:55,534
surveillance to ask, where is the vector occurring now?

4787
06:52:55,974 --> 06:52:59,694
And when these different sources of evidence are brought together, we can

4788
06:52:59,754 --> 06:53:03,334
begin asking the more important question: what has

4789
06:53:03,534 --> 06:53:06,174
changed? And what might that change mean?

4790
06:53:06,894 --> 06:53:10,374
So biodiversity informatics and vector surveillance are not

4791
06:53:10,434 --> 06:53:14,254
separate activities. Digitized voucher collections

4792
06:53:14,634 --> 06:53:17,794
can strengthen surveillance by improving identification,

4793
06:53:18,414 --> 06:53:21,234
comparability, and analytical readiness.

4794
06:53:21,854 --> 06:53:25,334
This reflects the established role of medical entomology

4795
06:53:25,394 --> 06:53:29,194
museums in preserving surveillance specimens and providing

4796
06:53:29,254 --> 06:53:33,074
reference material for accurate identification of

4797
06:53:33,114 --> 06:53:34,614
field-collected specimens.

4798
06:53:39,054 --> 06:53:42,614
Digitization is also particularly important when we look at

4799
06:53:42,734 --> 06:53:45,574
orphaned or under-curated collections.

4800
06:53:46,214 --> 06:53:50,194
These collections may contain irreplaceable specimens, but they can

4801
06:53:50,234 --> 06:53:54,034
be vulnerable because their associated information may be

4802
06:53:54,134 --> 06:53:57,094
incomplete or at risk of being lost.

4803
06:53:57,754 --> 06:54:01,554
Rescuing such a collection, therefore, involves much more than physically moving

4804
06:54:01,654 --> 06:54:04,514
specimens. We need condition assessment,

4805
06:54:05,054 --> 06:54:08,654
accessioning, curation, metadata recovery,

4806
06:54:09,114 --> 06:54:11,554
and integration into the museum database.

4807
06:54:12,134 --> 06:54:15,694
Because a specimen without its associated content

4808
06:54:16,334 --> 06:54:18,754
loses much of its scientific value.

4809
06:54:19,214 --> 06:54:22,674
Our approach can therefore be summarized in three statements:

4810
06:54:23,334 --> 06:54:24,614
preserve the specimen,

4811
06:54:25,934 --> 06:54:28,834
preserve the data, preserve the provenance.

4812
06:54:32,974 --> 06:54:35,454
A sustainable collection also depends on people.

4813
06:54:35,994 --> 06:54:39,914
The museum supports training in taxonomy and species

4814
06:54:39,974 --> 06:54:42,854
identification, curation and preservation,

4815
06:54:43,474 --> 06:54:46,534
georeferencing, data quality, and biodiversity

4816
06:54:46,654 --> 06:54:47,554
informatics.

4817
06:54:48,434 --> 06:54:52,254
In these pictures, we show some of our training and engagement activities.

4818
06:54:52,834 --> 06:54:56,074
Our collections support postgraduate students,

4819
06:54:56,894 --> 06:55:00,634
researchers, surveillance personnel, environmental health

4820
06:55:00,714 --> 06:55:02,694
practitioners, and public engagement.

4821
06:55:03,234 --> 06:55:06,694
This is important because digitization is not only a

4822
06:55:06,794 --> 06:55:10,554
technological process. We need people who understand the

4823
06:55:10,654 --> 06:55:14,574
specimens, their taxonomy, their history, and the quality of

4824
06:55:14,614 --> 06:55:17,814
their associated data. So sustainable means

4825
06:55:18,274 --> 06:55:22,214
preserving expertise as well as specimens.

4826
06:55:26,634 --> 06:55:30,414
So where are we going from here? Our priorities are to continue

4827
06:55:30,614 --> 06:55:34,154
and complete legacy digitization, strengthen

4828
06:55:34,234 --> 06:55:37,634
taxonomic verification, and georeference historical

4829
06:55:37,714 --> 06:55:41,334
records. We need to continue integrating

4830
06:55:41,514 --> 06:55:45,474
orphaned collections and improve interoperability between

4831
06:55:45,534 --> 06:55:49,434
our data and broader biodiversity information systems.

4832
06:55:50,054 --> 06:55:53,334
But ultimately, the objective is not simply to

4833
06:55:54,554 --> 06:55:56,414
have more records in our database.

4834
06:55:56,854 --> 06:56:00,494
The objective is to mobilize these datas for research and

4835
06:56:00,594 --> 06:56:04,134
surveillance. So the direction of travel is from

4836
06:56:04,234 --> 06:56:07,434
preserved heritage towards accessible,

4837
06:56:08,174 --> 06:56:11,954
reusable, and research infrastructure.

4838
06:56:17,754 --> 06:56:21,574
I would like to leave you with one central message, that natural history

4839
06:56:21,654 --> 06:56:23,714
collections are simply records of the

4840
06:56:25,374 --> 06:56:29,014
past. When historical specimens are properly curated

4841
06:56:29,554 --> 06:56:32,414
and their associated information is digitized

4842
06:56:32,922 --> 06:56:36,462
standardized and made accessible, they become reusable

4843
06:56:36,542 --> 06:56:39,982
scientific evidence. They can support taxonomy,

4844
06:56:40,562 --> 06:56:43,542
biodiversity research, environmental

4845
06:56:43,702 --> 06:56:45,842
interpretation, and vector surveillance.

4846
06:56:46,462 --> 06:56:50,442
For the Medical Entomology Museum, digitization does not replace the

4847
06:56:50,462 --> 06:56:54,302
physical collection. Instead, it renews the scientific

4848
06:56:54,442 --> 06:56:56,442
life of a century-old collection.

4849
06:56:56,942 --> 06:57:00,542
So our message is this: preserve the specimen,

4850
06:57:01,242 --> 06:57:04,582
mobilize the data, extend science.

