07:38:17,976 --> 07:38:20,956
of the organization and modified in the specimen record.

5040
07:38:25,336 --> 07:38:27,116
So are tools

5041
07:38:28,476 --> 07:38:32,056
such as these resulting in the enhancement of more existing

5042
07:38:32,136 --> 07:38:32,636
records?

5043
07:38:36,116 --> 07:38:36,776
Yes and no.

5044
07:38:38,776 --> 07:38:39,876
We're not seeing a lot,

5045
07:38:40,856 --> 07:38:43,536
but there is a larger impact in some years than in others.

5046
07:38:44,496 --> 07:38:48,456
So 2020, 2021, 2023 in

5047
07:38:48,496 --> 07:38:52,316
particular showed some more clearer trend to

5048
07:38:52,376 --> 07:38:54,176
increasing the completeness of the records.

5049
07:38:57,176 --> 07:38:59,776
Interestingly, the records submitted in 2016

5050
07:39:00,936 --> 07:39:04,576
fell from MIDS zero to MIDS minus one by 2018.

5051
07:39:05,216 --> 07:39:08,996
So when we looked into this to see what was happening, we found that five

5052
07:39:09,076 --> 07:39:12,856
datasets from a single institution accounted for a large

5053
07:39:12,896 --> 07:39:16,756
majority of these records. So for two years, these records

5054
07:39:16,796 --> 07:39:20,396
dropped the organization data in their submission to GBIF,

5055
07:39:21,036 --> 07:39:24,276
and then this seems to have been corrected by 2019.

5056
07:39:28,376 --> 07:39:32,136
So which data are proving to be most challenging to capture and share on GBIF?

5057
07:39:32,644 --> 07:39:35,804
And it depends on the taxonomic group, but in general,

5058
07:39:36,464 --> 07:39:38,404
the kind of preserved specimen,

5059
07:39:39,384 --> 07:39:41,364
some of the collecting event information,

5060
07:39:42,444 --> 07:39:45,844
media, geographic coordinates, and identifiers.

5061
07:39:47,144 --> 07:39:49,144
But if we look at the

5062
07:39:50,284 --> 07:39:53,184
three of the kingdoms in the

5063
07:39:53,244 --> 07:39:55,304
biological records

5064
07:39:57,744 --> 07:39:59,724
to highlight some of the elements.

5065
07:40:00,924 --> 07:40:01,924
If we look at

5066
07:40:03,624 --> 07:40:07,444
the object type, we can see that Animalia more often

5067
07:40:07,504 --> 07:40:08,404
have this recorded,

5068
07:40:09,484 --> 07:40:13,284
and this is probably caused by the higher variation in object

5069
07:40:13,364 --> 07:40:16,064
type in zoological specimens compared to herbaria.

5070
07:40:19,924 --> 07:40:23,704
Media is highest in Plantae, which is not surprising since these tend to be

5071
07:40:23,804 --> 07:40:24,804
simpler to image.

5072
07:40:27,404 --> 07:40:31,044
Collecting number is more likely to be present in Plantae and Fungi

5073
07:40:31,384 --> 07:40:32,344
than Animalia.

5074
07:40:33,824 --> 07:40:35,724
And finally, media ID

5075
07:40:36,803 --> 07:40:38,724
is higher in Plantae, and it's

5076
07:40:39,944 --> 07:40:43,044
possible that the implementation of the International

5077
07:40:43,724 --> 07:40:47,704
Image Interoperability Framework, IIIF,

5078
07:40:47,844 --> 07:40:49,684
is more common in herbaria.

5079
07:40:53,104 --> 07:40:54,524
So finally, some conclusions.

5080
07:40:55,444 --> 07:40:56,364
It's not straightforward.

5081
07:40:58,084 --> 07:40:58,324
It's

5082
07:41:00,384 --> 07:41:04,344
interesting that records in GBIF are not just becoming more complete, some are

5083
07:41:04,404 --> 07:41:05,384
decreasing too.

5084
07:41:06,984 --> 07:41:10,924
The impact of projects to enhance data can be seen, as we saw

5085
07:41:10,964 --> 07:41:12,064
with people identifiers.

5086
07:41:13,504 --> 07:41:17,424
The impact of GBIF making some data compulsory can be seen,

5087
07:41:18,604 --> 07:41:19,604
as we saw with license.

5088
07:41:21,424 --> 07:41:24,744
There are differences between kingdoms in terms of data completeness,

5089
07:41:25,804 --> 07:41:28,324
and these show different priorities and challenges.

5090
07:41:30,024 --> 07:41:32,464
And specimens are being enhanced over time,

5091
07:41:33,384 --> 07:41:37,164
with some cohorts of records in some years improving

5092
07:41:37,224 --> 07:41:38,144
faster than others.

5093
07:41:39,844 --> 07:41:42,504
And finally, big datasets make a big impact.

5094
07:41:45,184 --> 07:41:49,164
So the development of the MIDS standard is giving us a way now

5095
07:41:49,204 --> 07:41:52,724
to measure and monitor global digitization effort to see the

5096
07:41:52,804 --> 07:41:56,444
impact of all the work being done, and to help us check that the data

5097
07:41:57,524 --> 07:42:01,284
that we capture are being made available on platforms such

5098
07:42:01,504 --> 07:42:02,164
as GBIF.

