5187
07:46:03,494 --> 07:46:06,894
Today, I'm going to talk about the Latimer Core standard and the Global Registry of

5188
07:46:06,954 --> 07:46:08,074
Scientific Collections.

5189
07:46:09,114 --> 07:46:12,934
So first, for those who might not be familiar with GRcycle, I'm going to

5190
07:46:12,974 --> 07:46:16,934
explain a little bit what it is. It's a registry of physical scientific

5191
07:46:17,034 --> 07:46:19,934
collections and their associated institutions.

5192
07:46:20,554 --> 07:46:24,514
So it's really about the content of the collections, the specimens the collection

5193
07:46:24,554 --> 07:46:27,774
may hold, where the collection is located, who works there.

5194
07:46:28,334 --> 07:46:32,274
But it's also a repository for codes and identifiers.

5195
07:46:33,054 --> 07:46:35,914
It is curated by the community. So we have

5196
07:46:36,934 --> 07:46:40,834
more than 70, I think, editors who are currently

5197
07:46:40,874 --> 07:46:44,714
curating the information, but you can also make update suggestions if you

5198
07:46:44,774 --> 07:46:48,594
need. We also have and use external sources.

5199
07:46:49,134 --> 07:46:52,774
Index Perorum is actually the main source of data for

5200
07:46:52,814 --> 07:46:56,294
GRcycle. You can also link occurrences that are published on

5201
07:46:56,354 --> 07:47:00,254
GBIF to GRcycle entries, which allows us to make and

5202
07:47:00,354 --> 07:47:04,214
create dashboards. And in addition to that, GRcycle also

5203
07:47:04,254 --> 07:47:07,754
has an API, which allows programmatic access, but

5204
07:47:07,834 --> 07:47:09,354
also updates to the data.

5205
07:47:10,174 --> 07:47:13,334
And GRcycle can be embedded in the GBIF-hosted portals.

5206
07:47:13,974 --> 07:47:17,814
I won't go into details, but essentially, the current GRcycle website

5207
07:47:18,134 --> 07:47:20,014
is actually a hosted portal.

5208
07:47:20,954 --> 07:47:24,874
So on GRcycle, we have all kinds of data, and on one side you

5209
07:47:24,954 --> 07:47:28,434
have the collection textual information.

5210
07:47:28,674 --> 07:47:32,614
So it's really things like my collection contains

5211
07:47:33,154 --> 07:47:36,474
birds of North America or birds of a specific area, for example.

5212
07:47:36,674 --> 07:47:40,354
But on the other hand, you may have occurrences that are published on GBIF, and

5213
07:47:40,414 --> 07:47:43,434
occurrences are really at the specimen level.

5214
07:47:43,634 --> 07:47:46,754
It's the collection of a given organism at a given time and place.

5215
07:47:47,494 --> 07:47:51,274
The data in occurrences is highly structured, and

5216
07:47:51,494 --> 07:47:55,074
we'll be able to index it in GBIF and make it searchable.

5217
07:47:55,534 --> 07:47:59,374
You can search occurrences by country, dates, coordinates, and

5218
07:47:59,474 --> 07:48:01,034
so on. So

5219
07:48:01,874 --> 07:48:03,274
this is really great for indexing.

5220
07:48:04,114 --> 07:48:05,854
But sometimes you have something in between.

5221
07:48:06,794 --> 07:48:08,194
You have data about your collection.

5222
07:48:08,734 --> 07:48:12,574
It may be more than just a textual description, but you're not

5223
07:48:12,674 --> 07:48:15,254
yet fully at the digitized specimen level.

5224
07:48:15,394 --> 07:48:17,214
You're not yet at the occurrence level.

5225
07:48:17,294 --> 07:48:20,654
Or maybe you have a geological collection which cannot have occurrences on GBIF.

5226
07:48:21,774 --> 07:48:25,754
So for capturing the information in between, we created this data exchange

5227
07:48:25,794 --> 07:48:26,254
format,

5228
07:48:27,074 --> 07:48:30,654
that we named in the context of GRcycle collection descriptors.

5229
07:48:31,294 --> 07:48:35,254
So it's for things like, for example, list of collectors

5230
07:48:35,414 --> 07:48:39,154
or a list of taxa that you may have in your collection, or things that are

5231
07:48:39,574 --> 07:48:43,554
more than just the text, but not quite occurrences.

5232
07:48:44,094 --> 07:48:45,954
Collection descriptors are formatted like this.

5233
07:48:46,094 --> 07:48:50,054
You have tables, so it's flat tables, and the headers will tell

5234
07:48:50,174 --> 07:48:54,074
us what you put in the table. So you can choose to use Darwin

5235
07:48:54,154 --> 07:48:57,894
Core terms with the prefix WDC or Latimer

5236
07:48:57,994 --> 07:49:01,954
Core terms with the prefix LTC. You can also

5237
07:49:01,994 --> 07:49:05,874
choose to share unmapped columns, meaning you can put whatever you

5238
07:49:05,934 --> 07:49:09,754
want there and we'll be able to display it, but we might

5239
07:49:09,794 --> 07:49:12,394
not be able to recognize the content and index it.

5240
07:49:13,114 --> 07:49:17,074
So, for example, typically you will have things like custom statistics, like

5241
07:49:17,114 --> 07:49:20,054
the number of image specimen for a given taxon, for example.

5242
07:49:20,614 --> 07:49:24,554
So this we can display. It might be of interest for the users, but

5243
07:49:24,774 --> 07:49:28,094
it's not something that we can actually index and make searchable.

5244
07:49:29,214 --> 07:49:31,854
The Darwin Core terms and the Latimer Core terms, yes.

5245
07:49:32,154 --> 07:49:36,134
So a typical Darwin Core term that you could be using in your collection descriptor

5246
07:49:36,234 --> 07:49:39,534
is the scientific name. You can upload a list of scientific names for your

5247
07:49:39,574 --> 07:49:43,094
collection. Even if you don't have a fine resolution

5248
07:49:43,154 --> 07:49:46,974
identification for your specimens, you can still upload a list of taxa,

5249
07:49:47,674 --> 07:49:51,614
and then people who are looking for collections that may contain this taxa will be

5250
07:49:51,634 --> 07:49:55,514
able to find your collection. So I mentioned that Latimer Core terms can

5251
07:49:55,554 --> 07:49:57,154
be used in collection descriptors.

5252
07:49:57,934 --> 07:50:01,454
The two main ones that we're indexing currently are biome

5253
07:50:01,553 --> 07:50:03,494
type and object classification name.

5254
07:50:03,994 --> 07:50:07,854
And this is very helpful because it allows us to be able to index collections

5255
07:50:08,394 --> 07:50:11,534
based on things like whether they're an algae collection.

5256
07:50:12,294 --> 07:50:14,434
It's not a monophyletic group, algae.

5257
07:50:14,654 --> 07:50:18,154
So it's difficult to identify based on the scientific name, what

5258
07:50:18,194 --> 07:50:20,054
constitutes an algae collection.

5259
07:50:20,114 --> 07:50:23,474
But everyone knows whether they have algae in their collection, or at least should

5260
07:50:23,534 --> 07:50:23,694
know.

5261
07:50:24,554 --> 07:50:28,474
So now on GRcycle, we're able to search things like marine algae

5262
07:50:28,514 --> 07:50:29,974
collections for a given

5263
07:50:30,774 --> 07:50:34,494
country, for example. We use vocabulary when possible, and

5264
07:50:34,554 --> 07:50:38,074
Latimer Core helps align the vocabularies with what we use.

5265
07:50:39,314 --> 07:50:42,154
All the Latimer Core terms can be used to model descriptors.

5266
07:50:42,654 --> 07:50:45,074
However, we may not index everything.

5267
07:50:45,454 --> 07:50:49,414
The reason for that is that otherwise we'll have a lot of fields that

5268
07:50:49,474 --> 07:50:53,094
don't give any data. We need people to upload data to GRcycle first.

5269
07:50:53,194 --> 07:50:53,534
Otherwise,

5270
07:50:54,354 --> 07:50:55,814
it's a bit disappointing for users.

5271
07:50:56,094 --> 07:50:59,874
If you want a good example of a complete collection descriptor, you

5272
07:50:59,914 --> 07:51:03,754
can go to the Mace Botanical Garden page, and

5273
07:51:03,794 --> 07:51:07,254
these cofounders have uploaded data about the collection there

5274
07:51:07,634 --> 07:51:10,654
based on a survey that they ran a few years ago, I believe.

5275
07:51:11,534 --> 07:51:15,334
And they have modeled the data as collection descriptors and used

5276
07:51:15,374 --> 07:51:17,674
Latimer Core terms. So it's quite interesting to see.

5277
07:51:18,314 --> 07:51:22,014
Latimer Core is also used for a data exchange format

5278
07:51:22,414 --> 07:51:25,974
of GRcycle data. So basically, you can choose to

5279
07:51:26,014 --> 07:51:28,774
export collection or institution data.

5280
07:51:29,014 --> 07:51:30,434
You can also update GRcycle

5281
07:51:31,534 --> 07:51:34,794
with an import formatted as Latimer Core.

5282
07:51:35,374 --> 07:51:38,074
So it's quite helpful, but I'll talk about the limitations now.

5283
07:51:39,594 --> 07:51:43,314
So one limitation of our current implementation is that

5284
07:51:43,934 --> 07:51:47,914
there are lots of GRcycle terms, fields, that exist in our

5285
07:51:47,934 --> 07:51:51,794
database that don't really have an equivalent in Latimer Core, so we have to

5286
07:51:51,834 --> 07:51:53,554
model it as measurement of facts.

5287
07:51:54,542 --> 07:51:57,602
Which means that unless our end user

5288
07:51:57,702 --> 07:52:01,562
knows the measurement types we use, then they won't really be able

5289
07:52:01,642 --> 07:52:04,022
to format the data the same way we do.

5290
07:52:04,662 --> 07:52:08,342
So it's a bit of a limitation here. We're missing a bit the

5291
07:52:09,762 --> 07:52:12,282
terms there. Measurement effects can be a bit catchall.

5292
07:52:13,242 --> 07:52:16,662
Another thing that's limited in our current implementation is that

5293
07:52:17,202 --> 07:52:21,162
we're missing the collection descriptor part of the

5294
07:52:21,202 --> 07:52:23,302
institutions and collections in GR Cycle.

5295
07:52:23,482 --> 07:52:26,781
Well, institutions don't have collection descriptors, but the collections do.

5296
07:52:27,662 --> 07:52:31,002
And so it's a little bit too bad because it's missing from the export, meaning that

5297
07:52:31,102 --> 07:52:34,782
you don't get the full information when you're using the export format as Latimer

5298
07:52:34,882 --> 07:52:35,142
Core.

5299
07:52:36,402 --> 07:52:38,742
In addition to that, Latimer Core is changing.

5300
07:52:39,002 --> 07:52:42,182
The structure is going to be more formalized, so what we're planning for the

5301
07:52:42,242 --> 07:52:46,202
upcoming year is to revisit our implementation of

5302
07:52:46,242 --> 07:52:50,022
the Latimer Core export so that it includes collection descriptors and it is

5303
07:52:50,062 --> 07:52:53,282
compliant with the latest Latimer Core format

5304
07:52:53,342 --> 07:52:54,082
recommendations.

5305
07:52:54,902 --> 07:52:58,602
We also use Latimer Core in our teaching material.

5306
07:52:59,202 --> 07:53:02,742
So GR Cycle has a curriculum for editors, for those who want to be

5307
07:53:02,802 --> 07:53:06,522
national editors. And in that curriculum, we

5308
07:53:06,582 --> 07:53:08,622
have a collection modeling exercise.

5309
07:53:09,442 --> 07:53:13,382
It's a little silly fictional story of a university that

5310
07:53:13,422 --> 07:53:16,982
has a museum and also a teaching collection, and the

5311
07:53:17,022 --> 07:53:19,002
museum has several sub-collections.

5312
07:53:19,542 --> 07:53:23,402
And so we tell the story, and then we want people to think

5313
07:53:23,462 --> 07:53:27,422
about how they would model it in GR Cycle, but also in

5314
07:53:27,462 --> 07:53:31,202
Latimer Core. And so often the results are quite funny because

5315
07:53:31,242 --> 07:53:32,362
everyone has their own idea,

5316
07:53:33,262 --> 07:53:36,582
so it's a bit heterogeneous, and it shows also the flexibility of Latimer Core.

5317
07:53:37,242 --> 07:53:41,142
But it gives us a common language for modeling collection information, which is

5318
07:53:41,182 --> 07:53:41,722
quite nice.

5319
07:53:42,682 --> 07:53:46,382
And you can look at it here, the exercise, and also

5320
07:53:46,462 --> 07:53:50,262
the version generated by the Latimer Core maintenance group as

5321
07:53:50,302 --> 07:53:53,762
correction. And we tested this modeling exercise in a

5322
07:53:53,802 --> 07:53:57,782
DISCO-Flanders workshop earlier in May, and this was quite interesting

5323
07:53:57,822 --> 07:54:00,362
to see what everyone came up with. Thank you very much.

5324
07:54:00,442 --> 07:54:03,841
Thank you for the Latimer Core maintenance group and to people who work in GR

5325
07:54:03,922 --> 07:54:07,842
Cycle, on GR Cycle at GBIF, and all the GR

5326
07:54:07,902 --> 07:54:11,822
Cycle editors, and also the sources of information for

5327
07:54:11,862 --> 07:54:13,862
GR Cycle, including Index Herbarium.

