07:59:48,826 --> 07:59:51,666
What's happening is you're standing with the wrong remote. There we-- Yeah. Okay.

5434
07:59:55,626 --> 07:59:57,986
That one. I can see the remote. Here we go. Anyway. Come on. You can do it.

5435
07:59:58,186 --> 08:00:01,866
There we go. So Latimer Core is to groups as Darwin Core is to

5436
08:00:01,926 --> 08:00:04,586
individual occurrences, at its essence.

5437
08:00:05,686 --> 08:00:06,926
So that's what it is.

5438
08:00:08,986 --> 08:00:11,766
And we did a bunch of things through 2025.

5439
08:00:13,026 --> 08:00:15,166
As it stands right now, we have 15 open issues.

5440
08:00:15,366 --> 08:00:17,306
Some of them are bigger than others.

5441
08:00:17,406 --> 08:00:21,166
But the main thing that happened was that we had

5442
08:00:21,626 --> 08:00:24,186
a bunch of users and people in the community who said,

5443
08:00:24,206 --> 08:00:28,126
"This is a great thing, but we

5444
08:00:28,166 --> 08:00:32,046
need structure. We need you to tell us how to structure it so that we can implement

5445
08:00:32,106 --> 08:00:33,786
it." That was the big message that came through.

5446
08:00:34,366 --> 08:00:36,906
So we spent a lot of time this year trying to work out how to do that.

5447
08:00:37,546 --> 08:00:41,526
Part of it landed with we needed identifiers for things,

5448
08:00:41,926 --> 08:00:44,566
and we couldn't make it machine implementable without them.

5449
08:00:44,766 --> 08:00:46,086
So we spent a lot of time talking about that.

5450
08:00:47,246 --> 08:00:50,526
We do have two other places that are working with Latimer Core right now.

5451
08:00:50,566 --> 08:00:54,446
They haven't done anything public yet, but MCZ Base

5452
08:00:54,526 --> 08:00:57,786
and Elvis, we're talking with them about how to implement

5453
08:00:57,826 --> 08:00:59,626
Latimer Core. So it's happening.

5454
08:01:00,866 --> 08:01:01,126
And then we

5455
08:01:02,346 --> 08:01:05,946
did some disambiguation with the tag and with the

5456
08:01:06,006 --> 08:01:09,746
TDWG exec about what was the difference between an interest group, a task

5457
08:01:09,806 --> 08:01:12,766
group, a maintenance group, a working group, there's all this stuff.

5458
08:01:14,166 --> 08:01:14,746
And so

5459
08:01:15,686 --> 08:01:18,926
we figured that out. And then we started to really dig in

5460
08:01:19,906 --> 08:01:23,366
to the difference between structures and descriptions, and I'm going to

5461
08:01:23,786 --> 08:01:27,506
talk you through that and where TDWG has now landed in

5462
08:01:27,586 --> 08:01:31,306
terms of standards and defining them and what we have to do

5463
08:01:31,446 --> 08:01:34,986
in 2026. So this is a slicey bit in the middle.

5464
08:01:36,866 --> 08:01:39,346
Hopefully, I'm not teaching anyone things they don't know.

5465
08:01:39,506 --> 08:01:41,066
But I didn't know these things. So

5466
08:01:42,106 --> 08:01:45,986
we talk about URIs, we talk about IRIs, we talk about URLs.

5467
08:01:46,006 --> 08:01:48,586
There are all these weird acronyms with R in the middle.

5468
08:01:50,006 --> 08:01:53,166
And what I had to learn, what we had to learn was what the differences were.

5469
08:01:53,426 --> 08:01:57,026
So URIs, uniform resource identifiers, they kind of came

5470
08:01:57,086 --> 08:01:57,586
first.

5471
08:01:58,826 --> 08:02:02,786
They are a string of ASCII characters that allow you to identify something.

5472
08:02:02,866 --> 08:02:06,226
That's basically what that is. So when they talk about URIs, it's that.

5473
08:02:06,966 --> 08:02:08,346
So GUIDs are URIs,

5474
08:02:09,826 --> 08:02:13,706
UUIDs, DOIs, ArchiDs. If you look at them, you can often read

5475
08:02:13,766 --> 08:02:16,326
them. They're in ASCII. But then there's this IRI,

5476
08:02:17,846 --> 08:02:20,286
which is an internationalized resource identifier,

5477
08:02:21,366 --> 08:02:22,886
and that's got even more terms in it.

5478
08:02:22,946 --> 08:02:26,746
So you've now got a bigger pool of characters you can put together to uniquely

5479
08:02:26,826 --> 08:02:29,706
identify something. And this gets important down the line.

5480
08:02:30,126 --> 08:02:32,926
But I wanted you to know that they're not scary or weird.

5481
08:02:32,966 --> 08:02:33,366
They're just

5482
08:02:34,406 --> 08:02:37,626
strings of characters that are used to identify something.

5483
08:02:38,766 --> 08:02:42,326
Where they get interesting is persistence, right?

5484
08:02:43,346 --> 08:02:47,266
We talk about persistent URIs or persistent URLs or persistent

5485
08:02:47,366 --> 08:02:51,226
IRIs. A thing is only persistent if somebody keeps

5486
08:02:51,286 --> 08:02:51,406
it

5487
08:02:52,346 --> 08:02:55,166
in perpetuity and looks after it, and you can access it forever.

5488
08:02:55,186 --> 08:02:55,966
That's all that means.

5489
08:02:57,026 --> 08:02:57,646
And so the

5490
08:02:58,566 --> 08:03:02,246
issue with those is that someone has to take ownership for this string

5491
08:03:03,006 --> 08:03:05,486
so that it always exists, and it always means the same thing.

5492
08:03:07,086 --> 08:03:10,686
And then to make it really useful, there has to be a service, so that if you use

5493
08:03:10,726 --> 08:03:12,266
that string, it takes you somewhere.

5494
08:03:13,746 --> 08:03:16,106
And most of that costs money. Not all of it.

5495
08:03:16,506 --> 08:03:18,306
And there are increasingly things that don't cost money.

5496
08:03:18,366 --> 08:03:22,306
So this is often a big problem for an institution that can't-- Like,

5497
08:03:22,366 --> 08:03:25,806
we have 40 million things, and we still can't afford a persistent

5498
08:03:25,986 --> 08:03:29,426
URI, IRI, URL thing, string,

5499
08:03:30,366 --> 08:03:31,726
because we can't afford it, right?

5500
08:03:32,546 --> 08:03:35,546
But we had to work all of this out ourselves,

5501
08:03:36,686 --> 08:03:39,146
and once we figured this out, we're like, "Oh,

5502
08:03:40,586 --> 08:03:43,286
TDWG uses IRIs."

5503
08:03:44,346 --> 08:03:45,586
And when you become a standard,

5504
08:03:46,766 --> 08:03:49,426
you can have an IRI for all sorts of things,

5505
08:03:50,646 --> 08:03:53,046
and you can get them for what they call vocabularies.

5506
08:03:53,366 --> 08:03:53,666
So there's

5507
08:03:54,626 --> 08:03:55,986
a thing that looks like a URL,

5508
08:03:56,166 --> 08:04:00,306
http://rs.tdwg.org/ltc,

5509
08:04:00,606 --> 08:04:01,466
string of characters.

5510
08:04:02,418 --> 08:04:06,338
And it's an IRI. We put it in the IRI bucket, and

5511
08:04:06,378 --> 08:04:08,298
when you become ratified, you get one of those.

5512
08:04:08,818 --> 08:04:11,578
And so we ratified in Okinawa and we got one.

5513
08:04:11,618 --> 08:04:13,458
We were like, "Yes, we have this thing.

5514
08:04:13,498 --> 08:04:14,338
It's going to last forever."

5515
08:04:16,958 --> 08:04:20,758
The documents that TADWIG have also have IRIs,

5516
08:04:21,198 --> 08:04:24,718
and they have a slightly different format. So they're the rs.tadwig.org/.

5517
08:04:25,978 --> 08:04:29,378
Then there's the thing that goes with the standard, so ours would be LTC, and then

5518
08:04:29,398 --> 08:04:31,458
there's a document name, another string

5519
08:04:32,478 --> 08:04:35,618
that TADWIG looks after and makes persistent.

5520
08:04:36,458 --> 08:04:37,158
That's all good.

5521
08:04:38,258 --> 08:04:41,758
And then there's a third set of these IRIs, which are the terms.

5522
08:04:41,898 --> 08:04:44,178
And so a lot of you will have seen these with Darwin Core.

5523
08:04:44,218 --> 08:04:46,318
There's a URL that goes with every term.

5524
08:04:47,358 --> 08:04:50,318
This is where it got really complicated because they don't all mean the same thing.

5525
08:04:51,278 --> 08:04:51,598
So

