but there are some things that are more important in some disciplines than others.

5770
08:18:43,186 --> 08:18:47,166
So for example, information about the collector and the collection date is

5771
08:18:47,206 --> 08:18:50,706
considered more important in biology, whereas geology needs

5772
08:18:50,786 --> 08:18:54,686
those stratigraphic context fields to give you information

5773
08:18:55,166 --> 08:18:57,926
about where in the geologic record a sample is from.

5774
08:19:01,126 --> 08:19:04,206
I'm going to talk a little bit comparing these two systems.

5775
08:19:04,646 --> 08:19:07,146
To do that, I've lined them up as

5776
08:19:08,086 --> 08:19:11,406
I show here. So where a CEDARS score of three

5777
08:19:11,886 --> 08:19:13,546
aligns with a MIDS score of one.

5778
08:19:14,546 --> 08:19:16,906
The semantics here aren't perfectly aligned.

5779
08:19:18,106 --> 08:19:22,026
The Smithsonian system is based on this idea of a standard

5780
08:19:22,066 --> 08:19:25,946
record that you can be better than or a little bit worse than, whereas

5781
08:19:26,026 --> 08:19:27,966
MIDS is more about building out a record.

5782
08:19:28,686 --> 08:19:30,006
But this is reasonably close

5783
08:19:32,786 --> 08:19:34,266
for what I'm intending to talk about.

5784
08:19:37,446 --> 08:19:41,006
So first, I want to talk a little bit about how departments mapped

5785
08:19:41,366 --> 08:19:42,386
their individual data.

5786
08:19:43,686 --> 08:19:45,026
So this is a heat map.

5787
08:19:46,026 --> 08:19:49,906
On the y-axis, we have MIDS information elements that also

5788
08:19:49,966 --> 08:19:53,706
appear in the Smithsonian's scoring system for natural

5789
08:19:53,726 --> 08:19:57,586
history. On the x-axis is the CEDARS

5790
08:19:57,646 --> 08:19:59,106
level for those scores.

5791
08:20:00,566 --> 08:20:04,146
Brighter values mean that more departments have assigned a given

5792
08:20:04,206 --> 08:20:07,226
information element to a given score.

5793
08:20:08,506 --> 08:20:12,446
So in this case, in the leftmost column, you can see that the Name column is

5794
08:20:12,866 --> 08:20:16,466
bright yellow. That means that there's good agreement among departments

5795
08:20:16,866 --> 08:20:20,466
that that's a core piece of metadata that's absolutely required for a

5796
08:20:20,546 --> 08:20:21,286
standard record.

5797
08:20:22,346 --> 08:20:26,206
By contrast, most departments classified media as an

5798
08:20:26,246 --> 08:20:26,826
enhancement,

5799
08:20:27,886 --> 08:20:31,466
something you can use to create an above standard record, which is a little bit

5800
08:20:31,526 --> 08:20:35,506
different from the MIDS approach, where it's considered to

5801
08:20:35,526 --> 08:20:38,526
be a more fundamental part of the digitization process.

5802
08:20:42,126 --> 08:20:45,526
There's also some elements that appear in some department

5803
08:20:45,606 --> 08:20:48,786
scores that do not appear in MIDS, and those are primarily

5804
08:20:49,106 --> 08:20:52,686
internal data elements like storage location or

5805
08:20:52,746 --> 08:20:53,546
accession number.

5806
08:20:58,026 --> 08:21:01,766
When we compare the two scoring systems, we see some

5807
08:21:01,806 --> 08:21:03,366
significant differences between them.

5808
08:21:04,346 --> 08:21:07,926
So this is a plot of departmental CEDARS

5809
08:21:08,006 --> 08:21:11,586
scores and MIDS scores, and so it's the same underlying data

5810
08:21:12,666 --> 08:21:15,206
with the scores aligned as I discussed previously.

5811
08:21:16,066 --> 08:21:19,926
The black dots are the CEDARS scores, the white dots

5812
08:21:20,006 --> 08:21:21,006
are the MIDS scores.

5813
08:21:22,046 --> 08:21:25,846
And so what we can see on this plot is that there's a lot of variability

5814
08:21:26,646 --> 08:21:30,186
in these CEDARS scores because of how these rule sets have been defined,

5815
08:21:31,246 --> 08:21:35,026
whereas the MIDS scores cluster a lot more tightly around

5816
08:21:35,066 --> 08:21:35,406
three.

5817
08:21:36,446 --> 08:21:40,146
And so MIDS, because it's a consistent definition,

5818
08:21:40,666 --> 08:21:41,726
indicates that really,

5819
08:21:42,886 --> 08:21:46,126
this variability we're seeing in CEDARS doesn't really have a lot to do with the

5820
08:21:46,166 --> 08:21:46,946
underlying data.

5821
08:21:48,126 --> 08:21:51,766
By contrast, the MIDS scores are pretty tightly clustered

5822
08:21:52,066 --> 08:21:52,946
right around three

5823
08:21:54,046 --> 08:21:57,986
for CEDARS or one for MIDS, which means they might not be super

5824
08:21:58,046 --> 08:22:01,666
sensitive except to pretty big changes in the data

5825
08:22:01,706 --> 08:22:05,526
completeness. And that's largely to do with a big jump between

5826
08:22:06,266 --> 08:22:09,466
MIDS one and MIDS two scores in terms of the expected data.

5827
08:22:13,066 --> 08:22:16,366
Finally, I'm going to use the last couple minutes to talk about using metrics in

5828
08:22:16,426 --> 08:22:17,306
large collections.

5829
08:22:18,478 --> 08:22:22,398
I think there's a lot of potential for using something like MIDS or even our CEDARS

5830
08:22:22,498 --> 08:22:26,278
scores for organizing work in the collections, for deciding what

5831
08:22:26,337 --> 08:22:27,418
data's worth capturing,

5832
08:22:28,718 --> 08:22:32,698
what data might not be being exported when we submit data to GBIF or other

5833
08:22:32,758 --> 08:22:33,258
systems,

5834
08:22:34,357 --> 08:22:35,798
what records we need to improve.

5835
08:22:37,758 --> 08:22:41,158
And I think the way to approach this is less through the raw scores and more

5836
08:22:41,198 --> 08:22:41,538
through

5837
08:22:42,618 --> 08:22:43,438
some of the

5838
08:22:44,258 --> 08:22:45,198
element breakouts.

5839
08:22:46,498 --> 08:22:48,778
So this is a tool called MIDS Calculator.

5840
08:22:50,938 --> 08:22:54,918
This is available at the link below that's on the slide, and this

5841
08:22:54,978 --> 08:22:58,938
allows you to break out the specific elements that

5842
08:22:58,958 --> 08:23:00,958
are present in your records.

5843
08:23:01,838 --> 08:23:05,538
And so I've done that here for mineral sciences and paleobiology.

5844
08:23:06,158 --> 08:23:09,058
And so what I would say is that mineral sciences has

5845
08:23:10,478 --> 08:23:12,458
excellent completeness up through

5846
08:23:13,478 --> 08:23:17,458
MIDS one, but then drops off pretty significantly, particularly for stratigraphic

5847
08:23:17,538 --> 08:23:21,318
context, which is only present in a very small fraction of our records.

5848
08:23:22,757 --> 08:23:25,838
Whereas paleo has some issues with some of the core data.

5849
08:23:26,438 --> 08:23:28,978
For example, name and object type are

5850
08:23:32,158 --> 08:23:35,578
absent pretty frequently. License is a mapping issue.

5851
08:23:36,158 --> 08:23:37,178
Don't worry about that one.

5852
08:23:38,018 --> 08:23:39,418
But there is much better

5853
08:23:40,458 --> 08:23:44,338
coverage at higher levels for these geologic elements that are also part of the

5854
08:23:44,398 --> 08:23:44,818
standard.

5855
08:23:49,138 --> 08:23:52,918
I'm going to show two more plots. They're going to be heat maps like this one.

5856
08:23:53,018 --> 08:23:56,938
So this is a plot using the same color scheme as the

5857
08:23:56,978 --> 08:24:00,698
previous heat map. We have, in this case, the MIDS

5858
08:24:00,778 --> 08:24:04,698
information elements are on the X-axis, and the decade

5859
08:24:04,978 --> 08:24:08,418
at which the specimen was added to the collection is on the Y-axis,

5860
08:24:09,018 --> 08:24:10,458
and this is for the mineral sciences.

5861
08:24:12,038 --> 08:24:14,258
This is primarily to show that the metadata

5862
08:24:15,318 --> 08:24:17,738
is consistent in terms of when

5863
08:24:18,558 --> 08:24:22,458
material is added to the collection, so it's not like as we see more recent

5864
08:24:23,098 --> 08:24:25,278
acquisitions with much better metadata.

5865
08:24:26,258 --> 08:24:29,998
And also to show that the lack of stratigraphic context is

5866
08:24:30,638 --> 08:24:33,898
pretty much an intrinsic feature of the data that we're getting.

5867
08:24:34,018 --> 08:24:37,338
We just are typically getting between 5% and

5868
08:24:37,518 --> 08:24:38,338
20%, which

5869
08:24:39,558 --> 08:24:41,478
is actually significantly higher than the average.

5870
08:24:42,018 --> 08:24:45,838
So it's just very low, and it indicates that if we're going to use this for a

5871
08:24:45,878 --> 08:24:49,698
digitization project, we are at least internally going to need to settle

5872
08:24:49,818 --> 08:24:53,558
on some sort of system to either signify null

5873
08:24:53,618 --> 08:24:57,438
values that have been checked but no data is present or to just mark

5874
08:24:57,498 --> 08:25:00,798
records with an expected level of completeness.

5875
08:25:02,178 --> 08:25:04,458
And I know that this is stuff that the MIDS group has talked about,

5876
08:25:05,698 --> 08:25:05,938
but

5877
08:25:06,918 --> 08:25:09,278
in practical terms it's going to be very important for us.

5878
08:25:11,478 --> 08:25:15,278
And finally, I wanted to highlight some work that paleobiology

5879
08:25:16,058 --> 08:25:19,378
has done recently. I mentioned earlier that

5880
08:25:20,858 --> 08:25:24,838
the CEDARS statistics are tracked via what's called a primary

5881
08:25:24,918 --> 08:25:28,198
collection. So Paleo has recently revamped their collections

5882
08:25:28,898 --> 08:25:32,618
to focus more on manageable units of work that

5883
08:25:32,658 --> 08:25:33,478
needs to be done.

5884
08:25:34,858 --> 08:25:37,698
And so this is a sample of them. There are many more collections than this.

5885
08:25:38,438 --> 08:25:42,078
But some of the things that you can see on this diagram that might be helpful for

5886
08:25:42,158 --> 08:25:46,058
scoping work is one, for example, the name data,

5887
08:25:46,858 --> 08:25:50,638
where roughly 20% of Paleo's records are lacking a taxonomic

5888
08:25:50,678 --> 08:25:54,498
name. We can see that this is distributed largely in specific

5889
08:25:54,558 --> 08:25:55,098
collections,

5890
08:25:56,098 --> 08:25:57,578
which might be a good target

5891
08:25:58,458 --> 08:26:02,438
to work on. We can see that object type is pretty consistently absent,

5892
08:26:02,638 --> 08:26:06,258
and that's likely to be a mapping issue that can be addressed with some of the work

5893
08:26:06,318 --> 08:26:09,898
that Holly is doing and presented on earlier and will be presenting

5894
08:26:09,998 --> 08:26:12,538
on later in the conference on Thursday.

5895
08:26:14,058 --> 08:26:16,078
But also that there is significant variability

5896
08:26:16,998 --> 08:26:18,118
in many of these elements.

5897
08:26:20,658 --> 08:26:24,038
But I think this information level, element level view could be

5898
08:26:24,098 --> 08:26:27,458
useful for finding collections where we can

5899
08:26:28,278 --> 08:26:32,238
improve the quality of the records more easily and for targeting our work

5900
08:26:32,318 --> 08:26:32,858
in that way.

5901
08:26:34,058 --> 08:26:37,478
And that is all I have, so if you have any questions, I'm happy to take them.

5902
08:26:37,718 --> 08:26:38,658
And thank you for listening.

5903
